<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Easter</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pascha, Pasch, Resurrection Sunday</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Easter, also called Pascha or Resurrection Sunday, is a Christian celebration and cultural holiday commemorating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resurrection-of-jesus">resurrection of Jesus</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> describes as taking place on the third day after his crucifixion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at Calvary. It is preceded by Lent, a 40-day period of fasting, prayer and penance, and by Holy Week, and opens Eastertide, which lasts until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a>. A moveable feast, its date was set by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> (325) as the first Sunday after the first full moon on or after the vernal equinox. It is linked to the Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> by its name, its origin and much of its symbolism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/easter.jpg" alt="Easter" width="330" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Piero della Francesca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Resurrection.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Heavy taxation and the dominance of French officials provoked the Sicilian Vespers, the uprising that broke out in Palermo on <b>Easter</b> Monday, 30 March 1282, and massacred the French on the island.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Recorded contact with Europeans began on <b>Easter</b> Sunday, 5 April 1722, when the Dutch navigator Jacob Roggeveen reached the island and gave it its European name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Christian year is marked by Christmas, the birth of Jesus, and above all by <b>Easter</b>, his death and resurrection, preceded by the fasting season of Lent.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>They do not observe Christmas, <b>Easter</b>, birthdays or other holidays and customs they consider to have pagan origins.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jehovahs-witnesses">Jehovah's Witnesses · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Services are held on special request by a family, whether to celebrate a birthday, hold a memorial or funeral, cure illness, mark a school graduation or another significant life event, or keep a Christian holiday such as <b>Easter</b> or Christmas.</blockquote><a href="/religion/native-american-church">Native American Church · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>…expulsion of Ionan monks and clergy by Nechtan mac Der Ilei in 717 may have been linked to the dispute over the dating of <b>Easter</b> and the tonsure, where Nechtan seems to have supported Roman usage, or may have been meant to increase royal power over the church.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
